   <dc:title>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Exploratory Associations of Heart Rate and Running Velocity at Fixed Blood Lactate Concentrations with Sprint and Jump Performance in Male U19 Soccer Players</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">Exploratory Associations of Heart Rate and Running Velocity at Fixed Blood Lactate Concentrations with Sprint and Jump Performance in Male U19 Soccer Players</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:title>
   <dc:subject>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>blood lactate; countermovement jump; field testing; heart rate; soccer; sprint performance; U19</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:subject>
   <dc:type>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>Text</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:type>
   <dc:description>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Background: Relationships between heart rate and running velocity at fixed blood lactate concentrations with short-duration performance remain uncertain in U19 soccer players. This exploratory cross-sectional study examined their associations with 10 m sprint time and countermovement jump (CMJ) height. Methods: Sixteen male players from a U19 development squad completed CMJ and 10 m sprint assessments followed by an incremental field-running test. The best result from two CMJ and two sprint trials was analyzed. Pearson correlations were reported with 95% confidence intervals</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">Background: Relationships between heart rate and running velocity at fixed blood lactate concentrations with short-duration performance remain uncertain in U19 soccer players. This exploratory cross-sectional study examined their associations with 10 m sprint time and countermovement jump (CMJ) height. Methods: Sixteen male players from a U19 development squad completed CMJ and 10 m sprint assessments followed by an incremental field-running test. The best result from two CMJ and two sprint trials was analyzed. Pearson correlations were reported with 95% confidence intervals</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and the Benjamini--Hochberg procedure controlled the false discovery rate (FDR) across 24 tests. Results: Nominal positive correlations were observed between 10-m sprint time and heart rate at fixed blood lactate concentrations of 2.0--3.5 mmol$\cdotp $L$-$1 (r = 0.503--0.579; raw p = 0.019--0.047)</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and the Benjamini--Hochberg procedure controlled the false discovery rate (FDR) across 24 tests. Results: Nominal positive correlations were observed between 10-m sprint time and heart rate at fixed blood lactate concentrations of 2.0--3.5 mmol$\cdotp $L$-$1 (r = 0.503--0.579; raw p = 0.019--0.047)</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">indicating that higher heart rate was associated with longer sprint time. None remained statistically significant after FDR correction (q $\geq $ 0.207). No CMJ or running-velocity association survived FDR correction. Conclusions: The data did not provide multiplicity-corrected evidence of associations between fixed-concentration test responses and sprint or jump performance. The estimates are exploratory and require confirmation in larger</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">indicating that higher heart rate was associated with longer sprint time. None remained statistically significant after FDR correction (q $\geq $ 0.207). No CMJ or running-velocity association survived FDR correction. Conclusions: The data did not provide multiplicity-corrected evidence of associations between fixed-concentration test responses and sprint or jump performance. The estimates are exploratory and require confirmation in larger</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">prospectively powered samples.</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">prospectively powered samples.</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:description>
